  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "roles",
      "title": "A person’s procedural label can change",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "id": "c35d0c2e74b2600f2dfc5cfcb156e77455e7dcc967f573cee7dd9c611a5aeba2",
          "sha256": "b978f877ae368dbee23a70fc8326eb89beb23609fa557751631d394988c22c2b",
          "page": 1
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "At the first stage of the worksheet, the person bringing the claim is the plaintiff and the opposing party is the defendant. When a losing party seeks appellate review, that party becomes the appellant and the opposing party becomes the appellee. The labels describe roles at a stage of the dispute; they are not permanent personal characteristics.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "c35d0c2e74b2600f2dfc5cfcb156e77455e7dcc967f573cee7dd9c611a5aeba2",
              "sha256": "b978f877ae368dbee23a70fc8326eb89beb23609fa557751631d394988c22c2b",
              "page": 1
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "comparison": {
        "columns": [
          "Initial claim",
          "Appeal"
        ],
        "rows": [
          {
            "label": "Person seeking action",
            "values": [
              "Plaintiff",
              "Appellant"
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Opposing party",
            "values": [
              "Defendant",
              "Appellee"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "summary": "Plaintiff and defendant describe trial roles, while appellant and appellee describe the parties at the appeal stage."
    },
    {
      "id": "path",
      "title": "Follow the worksheet’s review path",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "id": "c35d0c2e74b2600f2dfc5cfcb156e77455e7dcc967f573cee7dd9c611a5aeba2",
          "sha256": "b978f877ae368dbee23a70fc8326eb89beb23609fa557751631d394988c22c2b",
          "page": 1
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The exercise models a path from a District Court to a Court of Appeals, followed by a petition asking the Supreme Court to accept the case. The worksheet names a writ of certiorari at that final request stage. A request for review and the court’s decision to hear the case are distinct events.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "c35d0c2e74b2600f2dfc5cfcb156e77455e7dcc967f573cee7dd9c611a5aeba2",
              "sha256": "b978f877ae368dbee23a70fc8326eb89beb23609fa557751631d394988c22c2b",
              "page": 1
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "title": "Initial claim",
          "text": "Identify plaintiff and defendant in the model District Court proceeding.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "c35d0c2e74b2600f2dfc5cfcb156e77455e7dcc967f573cee7dd9c611a5aeba2",
              "sha256": "b978f877ae368dbee23a70fc8326eb89beb23609fa557751631d394988c22c2b",
              "page": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Unfavourable decision",
          "text": "Separate disagreement with the result from the action needed to seek review.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "c35d0c2e74b2600f2dfc5cfcb156e77455e7dcc967f573cee7dd9c611a5aeba2",
              "sha256": "b978f877ae368dbee23a70fc8326eb89beb23609fa557751631d394988c22c2b",
              "page": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Appeal",
          "text": "Track appellant and appellee before the Court of Appeals.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "c35d0c2e74b2600f2dfc5cfcb156e77455e7dcc967f573cee7dd9c611a5aeba2",
              "sha256": "b978f877ae368dbee23a70fc8326eb89beb23609fa557751631d394988c22c2b",
              "page": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Petition for further review",
          "text": "The scenario asks the Supreme Court to accept review through a certiorari petition.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "c35d0c2e74b2600f2dfc5cfcb156e77455e7dcc967f573cee7dd9c611a5aeba2",
              "sha256": "b978f877ae368dbee23a70fc8326eb89beb23609fa557751631d394988c22c2b",
              "page": 1
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Follow the worksheet’s route from District Court to appeal, distinguishing a Supreme Court petition from acceptance of review."
    },
    {
      "id": "outcomes",
      "title": "Affirm, reverse and remand",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "id": "c35d0c2e74b2600f2dfc5cfcb156e77455e7dcc967f573cee7dd9c611a5aeba2",
          "sha256": "b978f877ae368dbee23a70fc8326eb89beb23609fa557751631d394988c22c2b",
          "page": 1
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "To affirm is to leave the lower court’s decision standing. To reverse is to reject or overturn that decision. To remand is to send the case back for further proceedings. A return to a lower court and a change in the decision are separate ideas; use the verbs precisely when following the worksheet.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "c35d0c2e74b2600f2dfc5cfcb156e77455e7dcc967f573cee7dd9c611a5aeba2",
              "sha256": "b978f877ae368dbee23a70fc8326eb89beb23609fa557751631d394988c22c2b",
              "page": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "key",
          "text": "The handout mixes patent and trademark wording and assumes a dated legal background. Practise the procedural vocabulary without treating those scenario assumptions as current legal advice.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "c35d0c2e74b2600f2dfc5cfcb156e77455e7dcc967f573cee7dd9c611a5aeba2",
              "sha256": "b978f877ae368dbee23a70fc8326eb89beb23609fa557751631d394988c22c2b",
              "page": 1
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Use affirm, reverse and remand precisely to distinguish leaving a decision standing, overturning it and sending a case back."
    }
  ],
